10	/**
11	 * A file manager, rather than a directory listing.
12	 *
13	 * What this replaces showed one flat list of the current directory with a
14	 * Rename and a Delete per row: no folder tree, no search, no multi-select, no
15	 * sorting, and an editor that opened as a modal over the top of everything.
16	 * The concept's Files screen is a three-pane browser, and every piece of it is
17	 * backed by an action that already existed.
18	 *
19	 * ONE BROWSER, TWO BACKENDS. This used to take a VPS `serverId` and call the
20	 * lib/actions-files server actions directly. It now takes a {@link FileApi} — a
21	 * data source — because the same screen has to drive two genuinely different
22	 * things: a customer's own VPS over SSH/SFTP as root (absolute paths on the box)
23	 * and a shared-hosting account over chrooted SFTP as the jailed account user
24	 * (paths relative to a docroot). Branching on "which product is this" inside a
25	 * component this size is how both versions drift; the adapters in
26	 * lib/files-adapters.ts absorb the difference instead, and everything below
27	 * works in this browser's own "/"-rooted virtual paths.
28	 *
29	 * The adapter also says what its source CANNOT do, and the controls that depend
30	 * on a capability are only rendered when it exists: the row Download link
31	 * appears only when `api.downloadUrl` is there. (`api.chmod` is optional for the
32	 * same reason — shared hosting has no chmod primitive — though this browser has
33	 * never rendered a permissions control for either backend.)
34	 *
35	 * DELIBERATELY ABSENT: move and copy. `rename` renames within the SAME parent by
36	 * design on both backends, and there is no action that relocates a path — so a
37	 * Move button would be a button that cannot work. Bulk delete is real (it is N
38	 * deletes) and is offered; bulk move is not.
39	 */
